<Base title="Strong Passwords, Explained" description="What actually makes a password strong: entropy, length, uniqueness — and why the password you invented is weaker than you think." path="/guides/strong-passwords-explained/">
  <nav class="breadcrumb"><a href={withBase('/')}>home</a> / <a href={withBase('/guides/')}>guides</a> / <span>strong-passwords-explained</span></nav>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Strong passwords, explained: length, randomness and uniqueness</h1>
    <p>
      "Use a strong password" is easy advice to give and surprisingly hard to follow, because
      most of what people believe about password strength is wrong. A password stuffed with
      symbols can still be weak, and a simple-looking one can be effectively uncrackable.
      Three properties decide which is which: unpredictability, length and uniqueness.
    </p>

    <h2>Attackers guess patterns, not characters</h2>
    <p>
      Nobody cracks passwords by trying aaaa, aaab, aaac. Modern attacks start with billions
      of passwords leaked from previous breaches, then apply the transformations humans
      reliably make: capitalise the first letter, swap a for @ and o for 0, append the year,
      append an exclamation mark — in that order, because everyone does it in that order. A
      password like Dragon2024! feels complex and is, to a cracking rig, one of the first
      few million guesses.
    </p>
    <p>
      The measure that cuts through this is entropy: how many equally likely possibilities
      a password was chosen from, expressed in bits. A password picked truly at random from
      a 94-character set gains about 6.5 bits per character, so length compounds fast. Twelve
      random characters are around 79 bits; sixteen are around 105. Each additional bit
      doubles the attacker's work, which is why one extra character beats any amount of
      clever symbol-swapping.
    </p>

    <h2>Length beats complexity — but randomness beats both</h2>
    <p>
      Given a choice, take length first: a long password is also easier to type and to make
      memorable in the form of a passphrase — several unrelated words joined together, chosen
      by dice or by software rather than by your sense of what sounds random, because human
      "random" word choices cluster heavily on the same few thousand favourites.
    </p>
    <p>
      The catch is that these numbers only hold when the choice is genuinely random. The
      moment a person picks the characters, the effective entropy collapses to whatever the
      attacker's dictionary already knows about people. That is the real argument for
      generating passwords with software, as the TextKit
      <a href={withBase('/tool/password-generator/')}>Password Generator</a> does: the output
      is ugly because it carries no pattern, and carrying no pattern is precisely the point.
    </p>

    <h2>A quick self-check</h2>
    <p>
      Take any password you currently rely on and ask three questions. Could a stranger who
      knows your name, your pets and your football team guess the ingredients? Has the same
      password — or a close cousin of it with a different year or symbol — ever been used on
      another site? And was it invented by a person rather than drawn at random by software?
      A "yes" to any of the three means that account is protected mainly by luck, and luck is
      the one factor that gets worse every year as breach databases grow.
    </p>

    <h2>Uniqueness: the rule people break most</h2>
    <p>
      Even a genuinely strong password becomes a liability when it is reused. Breaches are
      routine, and the first thing done with a leaked email-and-password pair is to try it —
      automatically, within hours — against banks, email providers and social networks. This
      is called credential stuffing, and it works because reuse is so common. One unique
      password per account confines any single breach to a single account.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody can memorise a hundred unique, random passwords, and nobody is meant to: that
      is the password manager's job. Remember one long passphrase for the manager itself,
      turn on two-factor authentication for your email and banking — the accounts that can
      reset all the others — and let software generate and store everything else. Strength,
      in the end, is a system, not a talent for inventing clever strings.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
